Subject: mmc: core: optionally send the eMMC to idle (CMD0) on restart

On a restart, mmc_shutdown() flushes the cache, sends a power-off
notification (POWER_OFF_LONG) and turns the host off. That assumes the
power goes away next. On the SM-G610F it does not: vmmc (LDO26) and vqmmc
(LDO27) are always-on, there is no power switch or pwrseq for the eMMC,
and RST_n_FUNCTION (EXT_CSD[162]) is 0, so hardware reset is not usable
either. The SoC is reset, the PMIC is not, and the boot ROM, the vendor
loader and U-Boot meet the device in whatever state Linux left it in,
including a high-speed timing (DDR52/HS200) and an outstanding power-off
notification.

With the opt-in property "mmc-go-idle-on-restart" on the host node, end
a restart (system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART, not a power-off) with the
host back at legacy timing, 1-bit bus and the identification clock,
followed by CMD0 (GO_IDLE_STATE) instead of the power-off notification.
CMD0 resets HS_TIMING, BUS_WIDTH and POWER_OFF_NOTIFICATION, i.e. the
device is in the same state as after power-up. The cache flush before it
is unchanged. A power-off still sends the notification.

Status: experiment for the boot-failure investigation (failures seen
after clean restarts from kernels running the eMMC at DDR52). Not proven
to change anything yet; see the test plan in the results file. Logs one
line per restart ("restart: device sent to idle (CMD0)").

Against: linux v7.3.4 + 0001-0017. License: GPL-2.0.

--- a/drivers/mmc/core/mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/mmc.c
@@ -2134,6 +2134,23 @@ static int _mmc_flush_cache(struct mmc_host *host)
 	return err;
 }

+/*
+ * Boards whose eMMC supplies are always on and which have no usable RST_n
+ * (RST_n_FUNCTION not enabled) never power cycle the device on a restart:
+ * the next boot stage finds it exactly as Linux left it. With the opt-in
+ * property "mmc-go-idle-on-restart" on the host node, a restart ends with
+ * CMD0 instead of a power-off notification.
+ */
+static bool mmc_go_idle_on_restart(struct mmc_host *host,
+				   enum mmc_poweroff_type pm_type)
+{
+	return pm_type == MMC_POWEROFF_SHUTDOWN &&
+	       system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART &&
+	       !mmc_host_is_spi(host) && host->parent &&
+	       of_property_read_bool(host->parent->of_node,
+				     "mmc-go-idle-on-restart");
+}
+
 static int _mmc_suspend(struct mmc_host *host, enum mmc_poweroff_type pm_type)
 {
 	unsigned int notify_type = EXT_CSD_POWER_OFF_SHORT;
@@ -2157,7 +2174,24 @@ static int _mmc_suspend(struct mmc_host *host, enum mmc_poweroff_type pm_type)
 			goto out;
 	}

-	if (mmc_card_can_poweroff_notify(host->card) &&
+	if (mmc_go_idle_on_restart(host, pm_type)) {
+		/*
+		 * VCC stays on across this restart, so a power-off
+		 * notification would never be followed by the power loss it
+		 * announces. Put the device back into the state the boot ROM
+		 * and boot loaders expect after power-up instead: host side
+		 * to legacy timing, 1-bit, identification clock, then CMD0,
+		 * which resets HS_TIMING, BUS_WIDTH and
+		 * POWER_OFF_NOTIFICATION on the device.
+		 */
+		mmc_set_timing(host, MMC_TIMING_LEGACY);
+		mmc_set_bus_width(host, MMC_BUS_WIDTH_1);
+		mmc_set_clock(host, host->f_init);
+		err = mmc_go_idle(host);
+		pr_info("%s: restart: device sent to idle (CMD0), err %d\n",
+			mmc_hostname(host), err);
+		err = 0;
+	} else if (mmc_card_can_poweroff_notify(host->card) &&
 	    mmc_host_can_poweroff_notify(host, pm_type))
 		err = mmc_poweroff_notify(host->card, notify_type);
 	else if (mmc_card_can_sleep(host->card))
